* bevy-explorer: the Bevy fork at dac1ac9 is vendored at rendering/bevy-explorer/bevy

Imported from https://github.com/robtfm/bevy, branch release-0.16-dcl, commit
dac1ac9ce08aa610e6f2aa7eef1e4e99130c196a (upstream bevyengine/bevy v0.16.1, 383b351,
plus 102 commits), through git update-index, so every blob is the fork's.

Left out, and why:
- assets/ (358 files, 55 MB): example assets no build or test reads; Bevy's own
  crates.io package excludes them too.
- examples/wasm/assets: a symlink into assets/.
- .gitattributes: its `* text=auto` would override this repository's -text rule
  for the subtree, which the root .gitattributes now sets.

Check: git diff --name-status dac1ac9^{tree} HEAD:rendering/bevy-explorer/bevy
lists exactly those 360 entries.

* bevy-explorer: the explorer builds Bevy from the vendored tree

[patch.crates-io] maps the 18 Bevy crates that ecosystem crates name by version
to paths under rendering/bevy-explorer/bevy, and a live
[patch."https://github.com/robtfm/bevy"] does the same for bevy_dui, bevy_ecss and
boimp, which name the fork by git. The commented local-path blocks go.

Cargo.lock changes only by the 47 Bevy packages losing their git source (and the 8
bevy_macro_utils references that disambiguated by it); every other package keeps its
version, checksum and dependencies. The resolved graph (crates, versions, features,
edges, dependency kinds) is identical before and after, for every platform and for
wasm32-unknown-unknown.

* bevy-explorer: the vendored Bevy allows the two lints Cargo no longer caps

A git dependency's rustc warnings are capped; a path dependency's are shown and
replayed on every build. dead_code (code the explorer's feature set leaves unused)
and mismatched_lifetime_syntaxes (newer than Bevy 0.16) were 810 of the 835 warnings
a workspace check printed; 30 others stay visible. This is the only change to the
fork's files.

* bevy-explorer: CI inputs, the web engine key and the fork inventory cover the vendored Bevy

- tooling/bevy-ci.py: rendering/bevy-explorer/bevy is an input of every lane and of
  the image consumer; a Bevy change selects the Windows and macOS matrix too.
- tooling/play-artifact.py and infra/packages/play.nix: Bevy's files are web engine
  inputs, in the git walk and in Nix's filtered contract, so a Bevy change misses the
